<header class="intro"><p class="kicker">2026 US FEDERAL INCOME TAX · FREE ESTIMATOR</p><h1>Marriage Tax Penalty or Bonus: <span>What Could Change?</span></h1><p>Compare two unmarried single returns with a married-filing-jointly return using 2026 IRS ordinary income tax brackets. This is an educational estimate, not tax preparation software.</p></header>
<MarriageTaxEstimator/>
<article class="guide"><h2>How does the marriage tax penalty calculator work?</h2><p>Enter annual ordinary income before deductions for both partners. For the single scenario, each person's income is reduced by their applicable assumed deduction, and 2026 single-filer brackets calculate federal income tax. For the married scenario, combined income is reduced by the joint deduction and the married-filing-jointly brackets calculate tax. The difference indicates a potential tax bonus or penalty in this narrow comparison.</p>
<h2>Example: Why partners' earnings matter</h2><p>Couples with significantly unequal earnings can sometimes benefit from the wider married-filing-jointly tax brackets. Couples with similar incomes can see a smaller difference or a penalty at certain income levels. This comparison does not incorporate every rule that changes upon marriage.</p>
<h2>What's excluded from these estimates?</h2><p>State tax, employment/payroll taxes, the net investment income tax, Medicare surtax, child and dependent credits, capital gains, additional deductions, alternative minimum tax, filing restrictions and other tax provisions. Itemized deduction inputs are supplied separately for single and married scenarios; the calculator does not determine their eligibility or limits.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>{faqs.map(([q,a])=><details><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}
